when we write transformations intially the spark will create a logic plan when actual trigger action(show,display,collect,write)happens
then catalyst will optimize and give you multiple physical plan and picks you a one plan which is low cost and spark creates a job for
it then Dag scheduler will further divided into stages and tasks Since the dag is acyclic the direction is one side not like a loop and
once every task is completed by the worker node result will get back to the driver 

1. Write transformations → Logical Plan built (lazy)
        ↓
2. Action triggered (show/display/collect/write)
        ↓
3. Catalyst optimizes → generates multiple Physical Plans → picks lowest-cost one
        ↓
4. Spark creates a Job for this action
        ↓
5. DAG Scheduler divides the Job into Stages (at shuffle boundaries)
        ↓
6. Stages divided into Tasks (one per partition)
        ↓
7. Tasks sent to Executors (worker nodes) → run in parallel
        ↓
8. Since DAG is acyclic → flow is one-directional, no loops back
        ↓
9. Once all tasks in all stages complete → results sent back to the Driver



